    "group": "Transition",
    "name": "transition-timing-function",
    "syntax": "transition-timing-function: ease | linear | ease-in | ease-out | ease-in-out | cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2) | steps(n, end);",
    "desc": "Sets the speed curve of the interpolation. Initial value is ease.",
    "example": ".fade {\n  transition: opacity 200ms ease-out;\n}\n\n/* overshoot: y values may leave the 0 to 1 range */\n.pop {\n  transition: transform 220ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);\n}\n\n/* quantised time, one jump per tick */\n.ticks {\n  transition: transform 1s steps(12, end);\n}",
    "lang": "CSS",
    "source": "content/ref/css-animation.json",
    "markdown": "`ease-out` decelerates into the destination and suits entrances. `linear` is correct for spinners and progress bars, where a varying speed reads as stutter. In `cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2)` the x values must stay between 0 and 1 because time cannot run backwards, while the y values may overshoot to create a bounce without keyframes.\n\n`step-start` equals `steps(1, start)` and `step-end` equals `steps(1, end)`.",
    "html": "<p><code>ease-out</code> decelerates into the destination and suits entrances. <code>linear</code> is correct for spinners and progress bars, where a varying speed reads as stutter. In <code>cubic-bezier(x1, y1, x2, y2)</code> the x values must stay between 0 and 1 because time cannot run backwards, while the y values may overshoot to create a bounce without keyframes.</p>\n<p><code>step-start</code> equals <code>steps(1, start)</code> and <code>step-end</code> equals <code>steps(1, end)</code>.</p>"
